  "designNotes": "Crude enters at the top, copper at the west, iron at the south, the chip leaves east and the gas disposal is at the bottom, so the two ends of the refinery net (crude, gas) are separated on the perimeter by the circuit sources: the gas line and the circuit line must cross, and belts cannot. The crossing is solved by merging gas and circuits onto one belt (merger) and splitting them again with a circuit-filter sorter, which swaps their sides. Everything else is packing and rate discipline: the chip assembler runs at 100% duty, the iron source is exactly matched to need (1 per 16 ticks) and the copper and crude sources have only about 15-20% slack. Solutions differ in cost (about 215 to 250) and first-delivery latency (about 72 to 105) depending on where the refinery, gadget and chip assembler sit and how far belts wander; footprint is pinned at the full interior by the ports on all four edges.",
  "par": {
    "footprint": 196,
    "cost": 216,
    "latency": 72
  }
